    {
      "id": "2116296706958798865",
      "author": "elonmusk",
      "author_name": "Elon Musk",
      "author_avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2103567689515954176/aR-Kjpp4_200x200.jpg",
      "author_verified": true,
      "author_verified_type": "individual",
      "created_at_utc": "2026-10-30T22:30:28.000Z",
      "created_at_shanghai": "2026-10-31 06:30:28",
      "type": "转发",
      "type_label": "转发",
      "text": "Our TPUs just phoned home... from space.\n\nICYMI, on October 1, we sent four TPUs into orbit to test whether our AI hardware could operate in space. The initial goal was to see if our chips could turn on, process a prompt, and transmit a response back to Earth.\n\nThis week, we got our answer. ✨\n\nThis milestone brings us one step closer to understanding if space-based compute is possible. We’re sending two more TPU-equipped satellites to orbit in 2027 for our next milestone.\n\nWatch this space 👀 and learn more about the first milestone of our long-term moonshot with Project Suncatcher ↓",
      "quote": null,
      "retweet": {
        "author": "NewsFromGoogle",
        "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1972717909559353344/eMJ6AJ0W_200x200.jpg",
        "created_at_shanghai": "2026-10-31 01:23:04",
        "created_at_utc": "2026-10-30T17:23:04.000Z",
        "id": "2116219345215074538",
        "images": [
          "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2116218853256515585/img/_yd2FyM786plp8Ox.jpg"
        ],
        "media": [
          {
            "duration": 82.499,
            "height": 1080,
            "thumbnail_url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2116218853256515585/img/_yd2FyM786plp8Ox.jpg",
            "type": "video",
            "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2116218853256515585/vid/avc1/1920x1080/Hc-_NO2scBfQyXnF.mp4?tag=29",
            "width": 1920
          }
        ],
        "name": "News from Google",
        "text": "Our TPUs just phoned home... from space.\n\nICYMI, on October 1, we sent four TPUs into orbit to test whether our AI hardware could operate in space. The initial goal was to see if our chips could turn on, process a prompt, and transmit a response back to Earth.\n\nThis week, we got our answer. ✨\n\nThis milestone brings us one step closer to understanding if space-based compute is possible. We’re sending two more TPU-equipped satellites to orbit in 2027 for our next milestone.\n\nWatch this space 👀 and learn more about the first milestone of our long-term moonshot with Project Suncatcher ↓",
        "url": "https://x.com/NewsFromGoogle/status/2116219345215074538",
        "verified": true,
        "verified_type": "organization"
      },
